{
  "id": "Compound::DB00782 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Propantheline, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, is shown to bind to Chrm2, which is involved in the activation of G-protein coupled receptors. This binding leads to the participation of Chrm2 in various receptor activities, including muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity and G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity. These activities ultimately result in the participation of Chrm1 in the biological process of muscarinic receptor signaling. The evidence suggests that Propantheline exerts its effects by inhibiting the muscarinic acetylcholine receptor, which is a well-documented mechanism of action in pharmacology. The integration of multiple paths highlights a consistent relationship between Propantheline and Chrm1, supported by overlapping pathways involving G-protein coupled receptors. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a direct binding interaction and the involvement of Chrm1 in the receptor activity network, reinforcing the role of Propantheline in modulating muscarinic signaling.",
